in november the small city of merit bc  awoke to this  the aftermath of an atmospheric river  that unleashed a torrent of rain  in a matter of hours the cold water  river that runs alongside the city  breached its banks washing away roads  bridges and homes  the force of the river grew so powerful  that it carved a new route through the  city a key water main burst and merit's  waste water treatment plant quickly  became inundated by the raging river  with no running water an evacuation  order went out for the entire city of  seven thousand people  when the rain finally stopped two weeks  later many residents returned to find  their homes devastated  a thick layer of silt coated everything  basements streets and even the city's  wastewater treatment plant located not  far from the river's banks  this was all flooded out the day of the  event  so during the event  our basement was filling up with water  from coming through our manholes and  running in through this chamber here and  it came up to the stair just level with  the wall there  the chief water operator here in merit  is kevin velak  he's been working alongside the public  works director to clean up the plant  the worst flood i've been through prior  to this was the flood in 2018 from the  nicola river  and in comparison  [Music]  it was nothing it was a mere trickle  compared to what we just went through  [Music]  there could be many merit bc's in canada  those flood events could occur in  southern saskatchewan or southern  ontario or new brunswick pretty much  anywhere blair feltmate studies how to  prepare communities for the more severe  weather of the future in industry lingo  it's called climate change adaptation  so adaptation is basically putting the  measures in place that make it such that  when the the big events occur the floods  the fires the hail storms wind storms  whatever it might be that the amount of  damage realized due to those events is  very much minimized because we've put  the measures in place to to keep the  damage in check climate change is  causing rising sea levels and more  intense storms making flooding the  biggest and most costly threat to canada  at risk is the country's most important  infrastructure  that's not just our roads and railways  it's electricity fuel water and food  supply the basic necessities of life  that  is mission critical to the fact that if  you're going to remain in the community  that stuff has to run if it doesn't run  you have to leave  when there's a flood municipalities and  provinces react  but what about preventative measures  like flood-proofing buildings and  essential services most of that falls on  local governments to plan and fund  the problem is those things come with a  steep price tag  linda brown is the mayor of merit and  she knows that most municipalities  especially smaller ones like hers can't  afford it  we're talking bridges  wastewater treatment plants those two  items alone are probably 30 40 million  so there's a whole lot of  dollars that we can't even fathom at  this point  whatever federal funding is available is  limited and competitive and that's led  to a patchwork of preparedness across  the country  the problem in canada on climate change  has been that up until just recent years  98 of discussion on the climate file has  focused on mitigating greenhouse gas  emissions which is the smart thing to do  and i'm fully in favor of that but we've  done so to the exclusion of talking  about adaptation  even in the absence of national  coordination a few cities have started  taking adaptation seriously  so there was a huge storm system that  moved through edmonton in 2004 and  caused massive flooding especially in  the southwest part of the city here  my parents were out of town and so i  rushed over here and  streets were flooded people were  actually canoeing out on the main road  behind my parents place  alberta's capital is prone to flooding  don iveson is the former mayor for years 